Below is a step‐by‐step breakdown of the mission design, followed by a detailed goal–model decomposition and the corresponding task definitions. The design considers that the food‐delivery system must decide between two modes: tray retrieval (which requires cooperation with an inpatient, companion, nurse, or another robot) and direct table delivery (which uses a special manipulation skill). In parallel, the system must also retrieve dirty dishes from the room using one of several cooperative methods and, if needed, open the room door (possibly with external assistance).

─────────────────────────────  
Step 1. Top‐Level Analysis

• The mission has two main high-level functions:  
 – Food delivery from the kitchen to an inpatient room  
 – Retrieval of dirty dishes (and possibly door opening) from the room

• For food delivery, the system must decide between:  
 – Tray retrieval (preferred if the inpatient, companion, or nurse is able)  
 – Direct table delivery (fallback if tray retrieval is not possible)

• The decision is made by querying the inpatient record for tray retrieval capability. Based on the query result, the system “falls back” to the appropriate delivery mode.

• For dish retrieval, multiple cooperative strategies are available (unassisted, two robots, or robot–human cooperation). In addition, door opening may be required if someone in the room calls for dish pickup. Here too the system may need to query whether external assistance is available.

