3. Logical Relationships Summary

The following table summarizes how the goals and tasks relate to one another:

-----------------------------------------------------------
| Logical Relationship                                 | Justification                                                                                                                                              |
|------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| G1 → G1.1, G1.2, G1.3 [Fallback]                      | “G1 selects the delivery method based on tray retrieval capability (G1.1); if available, G1.2 (tray delivery) is executed; otherwise, G1.3 (direct table delivery) is used.” |
| G1.2 → G1.2.1, G1.2.2, G1.2.3, G1.2.4 [AND]           | “Sequential steps are required for successful tray delivery: coordination, meal indication, tracking, and error alerting.”                                     |
| G2 → G2.1, G2.2 [AND]                                 | “Both dish retrieval and door opening operations are necessary for the mission; they are executed concurrently (in parallel) when triggered.”                    |
| G2.2 → G2.2.1, AT9 [Fallback]                         | “If door opening assistance is available (G2.2.1), cooperation is used; otherwise, AT9 is executed for door opening.”                                         |

─────────────────────────────  
In summary, the multi‐robot mission is decomposed into two main high-level goals. For food delivery (G1), the system queries the inpatient record (G1.1) to decide between tray retrieval (G1.2) and direct table delivery (G1.3). The tray retrieval method further breaks down into sequential steps (G1.2.1–G1.2.4) to ensure proper coordination, correct meal indication, logging, and error alerting. For dish retrieval (G2), the system selects among three cooperative methods (G2.1 with alternatives G2.1.1–G2.1.3) and, if required, opens the room door (G2.2) after querying for external assistance (G2.2.1), with a fallback to an unassisted door opening (AT9) if necessary.

Each of these goals and tasks is designed to reflect the mission’s inherent uncertainty (e.g., availability of a companion, human assistance, and cooperation between robots) and the need for coordination across different operational modes.

